[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering HCL Syntax and Language Features in Terraform"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL) is the declarative language used by Terraform to provision and manage cloud infrastructure. Designed to be both human-readable and machine-friendly, HCL bridges the gap between complex infrastructure definitions and developer usability."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Syntax Elements"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At its core, HCL relies on blocks, arguments, and expressions to define infrastructure resources. Understanding these components is critical for writing clean, maintainable Terraform configurations."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Blocks: Containers for other content, usually representing a specific object like a resource, data source, or module. They have a block type, zero or more labels, and a body."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arguments: Assigned within a block using the format name = value, defining properties of that object."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Expressions: Represent a value either literally or by referencing other values through functions, operators, and variables."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"resource "aws_instance" "web_server" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = "ProductionWebServer"
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Types and Expressions"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"HCL supports multiple scalar and complex data types. Scalars include strings, numbers, and booleans. Complex types include lists, maps, sets, and objects, enabling sophisticated structuring of configurations."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"variable "availability_zones" {
  type    = list(string)
  default = ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b"]
}

variable "server_config" {
  type = map(any)
  default = {
    cpu    = 2
    memory = "4GB"
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Expressions and Functions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Terraform provides built-in functions for manipulating data, alongside ternary operators for conditional logic. The syntax for a conditional expression is condition ? true_val : false_val."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = var.custom_ami != "" ? var.custom_ami : var.default_ami
  instance_type = lookup(var.instance_sizes, var.environment, "t2.micro")
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for HCL Development"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always format your code automatically by running the terraform fmt command before committing."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use descriptive resource names and adhere to a consistent naming convention across your modules."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage validation blocks inside variables to catch misconfigurations early during the planning phase."}]
